WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.910
The framework inserts one extra step into the usual
data-sharing pipeline.

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:07.069
Instead of merging raw datasets,

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:13.745
each peer first passes its features through an
autoencoder to obtain a latent-space representation,

00:00:13.825 --> 00:00:17.319
an obfuscated version of the data ready to be shared.

00:00:17.399 --> 00:00:23.389
The peers then join these representations using a
shared observation ID and train a single

00:00:23.469 --> 00:00:28.177
supervised model on the combined embeddings to
predict the same target.

00:00:28.257 --> 00:00:33.909
The autoencoder is fixed across all experiments so
conclusions reflect the framework,

00:00:33.989 --> 00:00:40.721
not network tuning: the encoder has four layers
going from N input features through hidden sizes 128,

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:46.318
64, and 40 down to an embedding of size M, and the
decoder mirrors it back up to N.

00:00:46.398 --> 00:00:50.027
Every layer uses ReLU, the loss is Mean Absolute Error,

00:00:50.107 --> 00:00:54.613
and training uses the Adam optimizer with a
learning rate of 0.0001.

00:00:54.693 --> 00:00:56.434
In the non-naive scenarios,

00:00:56.514 --> 00:01:02.234
the autoencoder becomes a multitask network that
also predicts the objective variable,

00:01:02.314 --> 00:01:06.280
so the encoding is guided by the downstream
prediction task.
